      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {acceptedTable.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{row.type}</td>
            <td>{row.buyback_likelihood}</td>
            <td>{row.notes}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How the process works</h2>
    <ol class="how-to-list">
      {howToSteps.map((s) => (
        <li><strong>{s.name}.</strong> {s.text}</li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Which types most often qualify for buyback</h2>
    <p>Not all computers qualify. Rough guide:</p>
    <p>Almost always qualify: recent MacBooks (last 3-4 years), recent workstations with meaningful GPU/CPU specs, recent gaming laptops with discrete graphics, business laptops from the last 3 years.</p>
    <p>Sometimes qualify: consumer laptops from the last 3-4 years (depends on brand and specs), desktop towers with recent CPU/GPU, all-in-ones from the last 3 years, enterprise servers with current-generation CPUs.</p>
    <p>Rarely qualify: consumer desktops 5 years old or older, business machines 6 years old or older, older MacBooks (7 years old or older), machines with heavy visible wear or non-working components.</p>
    <p>Don't qualify: non-working machines, physically broken machines, machines with major cosmetic damage, obsolete-generation hardware.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Workstations, servers, and specialised hardware</h2>
    <p>Workstation-class machines — Dell Precision, HP Z, ThinkPad P — often retain value longer than consumer machines because they have a specific enterprise buyer market. Same for gaming laptops with discrete GPUs (RTX cards especially) and video-editing workstations with high-end CPU/GPU/RAM configurations.</p>
    <p>Enterprise servers are more niche. Buyback exists but the market is narrower and quotes vary widely by generation. Send full model info (Dell PowerEdge Rxxx, HPE ProLiant DLxxx, Cisco UCS Cxxx) plus CPU generation and RAM for the specific estimate.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Deciding: sell or recycle</h2>
    <p>Rough rule of thumb: if the machine is under 5 years old, works, and was reasonably specced when new, get a buyback estimate before assuming it's recycling-only. Estimates are free and non-committal.</p>
    <p>If the machine is over 8-10 years old or doesn't work, it's almost certainly recycling. That's not a bad outcome — free pickup and proper material recovery is still worth doing.</p>
    <p>The /tools/sell-or-recycle-decision-tool/ walks through this without needing to know current pricing.</p>
  </section>

